#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092028 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b03546f-6c79-53b9-b284-fb23ae4dba52" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "1957007f-eabd-5d40-8f59-ff635ea1d539" :question/math-academy-id "e-602" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010979/assets/834eff403ac2a6f696e104ec914cac1b951f8a6d989e11c0e9f2576ac9e7f0d8.png)\n\nFind an integral expression for the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=1-t, \\quad y= \\dfrac{2}{3} t^{3/2}, \\qquad t\\in[0,1].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{ \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-t)=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{2}{3}{t}^{3/2})={t}^{1/2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{(-1)}^{2}+{({t}^{1/2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092029 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c82bbc8-83bc-55f8-be32-4d63f6db7261" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "86835544-a7f1-5be2-99c1-14e39a14f3fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-603" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve defined parametrically as\n\n$$\nx=e^t(\\cos{t}+\\sin{t}), \\quad y=e^t(\\cos{t}-\\sin{t}) , \\quad t\\in \\left[0,\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}\\right].\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010979/assets/15d185178bf8882f837c19361d630c0e537e145ca2337cad4655677255d0348a.png)" :question/worked-solution "Computing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+{e}^{t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)) \\\\  & ={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)+{e}^{t}(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t) \\\\  & =-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)}^{2}+{(-2{e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{4{e}^{2t}({cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}2{e}^{t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{e}^{t}{|}_{0}^{\\pi /4} \\\\  & =2({e}^{\\pi /4}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186092060 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebd2a5c8-5d15-589d-81c9-9b67f4e8f0b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve Containing Special Functions"}] :question/id #uuid "5bae6269-2112-56ea-b123-bee58701cf87" :question/math-academy-id "e-7130" :question/problem "Find the expression that gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=t, \\, y=\\ln{(\\cos{t})}$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\dfrac{\\pi}{4}$ (see graph below).\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010979/assets/48de26c11ae08afb45ec19ee56e770f7418f9be2afb8ee7dd46a0d1136cb125b.png)" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{t_1}^{t_2} \\sqrt{ \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2 + \\left( \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\:\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t))=-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(-\\operatorname{tan}⁡t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+{tan}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\operatorname{sec}⁡t\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289504 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebd2a5c8-5d15-589d-81c9-9b67f4e8f0b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve Containing Special Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b8469ff2-4b08-594f-b14a-113d5edfa378" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)+36\\operatorname{cos}⁡(6t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289506} {:answer/id #uuid "d1082dc3-8db8-5413-9f03-5ed42be40922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{5{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+6{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289507} {:answer/id #uuid "c6474247-b26b-52e8-bc5c-50b7671013e7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{10{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+12{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289508} {:answer/id #uuid "2ffecb6a-2f93-5a53-8042-e998df56cb5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+36{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289509} {:answer/id #uuid "5757d727-5d80-5732-ac89-35e82d0c08ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+36{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289510}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5757d727-5d80-5732-ac89-35e82d0c08ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+36{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289510} :answer-field/id #uuid "1329b6ad-536d-528f-b20e-bd9707ff3ace" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289505}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "30eadd5a-469c-54ec-b683-aec3a996fde4" :question/math-academy-id "q-123030" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14010979/assets/8868658c9bc7195f435a0109fce26b8a4fa39324c0a9a39e3fb644c5f02cdc9b.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t),\\,y=6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ (shown above)?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t))=-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(6\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))=6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(-5\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t))^{2}+(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{25{sin}^{2}⁡(5t)+36{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289518 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b03546f-6c79-53b9-b284-fb23ae4dba52" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7352711a-225c-5f90-b63a-dc36a885a0b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+2t+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289520} {:answer/id #uuid "e6c4b173-b925-5e7f-bd18-48f10587dd2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{9{t}^{2}+4}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289521} {:answer/id #uuid "30f487a7-9c66-5fa1-b725-bf167f8136c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{18{t}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289522} {:answer/id #uuid "e22e7a8c-23a9-5fd4-87a1-c8b5b2ac2d88" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{3{t}^{2}+2}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289523} {:answer/id #uuid "ea76e21e-2b69-535e-81a9-5e3718e47e23" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{18{t}^{2}+24t+8}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289524}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30f487a7-9c66-5fa1-b725-bf167f8136c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{18{t}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289522} :answer-field/id #uuid "6623aab4-4a58-5efe-bc40-75147a9ee4bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289519}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dddf8a10-d360-5144-aae4-5bd6e2bd6915" :question/math-academy-id "q-15174" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x={t}^{3}+{t}^{2},\\,y={t}^{3}-{t}^{2}$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=1$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}+{t}^{2})=3{t}^{2}+2t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{3}-{t}^{2})=3{t}^{2}-2t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{(3{t}^{2}+2t)}^{2}+{(3{t}^{2}-2t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{9{t}^{4}+12{t}^{3}+4{t}^{2}+9{t}^{4}-12{t}^{3}+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{18{t}^{4}+8{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\sqrt{18{t}^{2}+8}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebd2a5c8-5d15-589d-81c9-9b67f4e8f0b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve Containing Special Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5920438b-2e3b-541f-ab4e-274254545ac7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289534} {:answer/id #uuid "399e6721-a202-5532-b4d2-8072febd6298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289535} {:answer/id #uuid "dc35f953-44cc-5a97-9b01-08b169583ffb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)-4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289536} {:answer/id #uuid "3dd93796-2a27-5af2-9fd1-3d6111d06371" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+2{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289537} {:answer/id #uuid "dbe1ff85-e1c9-55a4-ac69-c135a08bf481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{6{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)-2{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289538}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "399e6721-a202-5532-b4d2-8072febd6298" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289535} :answer-field/id #uuid "c5703905-bd65-5e76-8460-7fa1094f93c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289533}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "eb7dbcac-2317-56a8-ba31-4c6afb0fd698" :question/math-academy-id "q-21339" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-1,\\,y=2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t)-1) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t)+2) \\\\  & =-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{(6\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t))^{2}+(-4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{36{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =2{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡(3t)+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289539 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "ebd2a5c8-5d15-589d-81c9-9b67f4e8f0b3" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve Containing Special Functions"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e5d95e76-6da0-59e7-ba2e-e54f6c229bf5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289541} {:answer/id #uuid "f95a3754-8e7a-5ac7-91b2-3967462239a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289542} {:answer/id #uuid "b72d03d8-d86a-5b26-b400-5fee85bd9c6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289543} {:answer/id #uuid "874c357f-f834-5902-ac5e-24d20b485ce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289544} {:answer/id #uuid "2d21e6b1-f959-5937-95f2-768406814b64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289545}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "874c357f-f834-5902-ac5e-24d20b485ce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289544} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd5d6221-d149-530f-a681-b77ad15e3700" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289540}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "78d28f04-080c-597a-9016-2f2665e007f7" :question/math-academy-id "q-21354" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t),\\,y=t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$\nfrom $t=0$ to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{4}$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t))^{2}+(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t))^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡(t)+{sin}^{2}⁡(t)+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)+1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{1+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)+1+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /4}\\sqrt{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t)-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)+{cos}^{2}⁡(t)}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186289556 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b03546f-6c79-53b9-b284-fb23ae4dba52" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b9ce05e-feb5-5fe3-870a-a219dc000627" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{t+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289558} {:answer/id #uuid "128635bd-e3aa-51de-a4b1-ac8a3bc5a04e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289559} {:answer/id #uuid "1adb0459-0306-56c4-9603-05ee11cc0723" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{4+4t+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289560} {:answer/id #uuid "dcedce22-bb8c-59a9-9aea-2d9251e58f9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4t+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289561} {:answer/id #uuid "0bb7e14b-c95b-5a70-a3cc-a409e14ba364" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{4+t+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "128635bd-e3aa-51de-a4b1-ac8a3bc5a04e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289559} :answer-field/id #uuid "991f5431-88a0-5a20-97fb-1e3934cbfd5c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289557}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "770bbdf1-cce0-50f8-8d53-5bed9c9a42c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-81377" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve defined by the parametric equations\n$x=t-1,\\,y={t}^{2}+2$\nfrom $t=-1$ to $t=2$ ?" :question/worked-solution "To compute the arc length $L$ of a parametrically defined curve, we use the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\nComputing the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t-1)=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({t}^{2}+2)=2t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe substitute these derivatives in the formula for arc length, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{{t}_{1}}^{{t}_{2}}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}+{(\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{{(1)}^{2}+{(2t)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+4{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
